# Gate expectations for Rust — the hand-written ground truth.
#
# Every line below was written by reading the fixture sources in `src/`, not by
# reading the engine's output. A line is a claim about what the source says, and
# the gate measures the engine against it. If a line here is wrong, the fix is to
# correct this file and say why in the commit; changing it to match the engine
# turns the gate into a snapshot of whatever the engine happens to do, which is
# the thing this crate exists to prevent.
#
# Three of the labels in this file were found to be missing by the first run of
# the gate — four parameters, one import and one call site — and they are in the
# fixture's source, so they are in this file now. The engine's score for them is
# reported as it fell out, not adjusted.
#
# **When a line here is corrected.** A line is corrected when this file contradicts
# itself or when it contradicts the source, and then only with the reason written
# next to it. A line the engine merely *misses* is reported rather than changed:
# changing a line because the engine cannot produce it is how a gate becomes a
# snapshot of whatever the engine happens to do, which is the thing this file
# exists to prevent. Three lines were corrected when reference extraction became
# reachable, and one line was left in place on purpose:
#
#   * `no_reference | src/lib.rs | summarise | entries` became
#     `no_reference | src/lib.rs | summarise.entries | entries`, and
#     `reference | src/lib.rs | summarise | entries` was added. `entries.iter()` is
#     a use of `entries` in the body of `summarise`, and this file already says a
#     use of a name is a reference wherever it appears — and already labels the
#     identical construct as one at `src/model.rs` (`entry | label`). The old line
#     forbade that use, so it could not be true at the same time as those.
#   * `no_reference | src/lib.rs | counted | counted` was replaced by
#     `no_reference | src/lib.rs | outer.inner.deep | deep`. `counted!(body())` is
#     an invocation of a name declared at the top of the file, not the function's
#     own name, and this file says elsewhere that a callee is a use of its name —
#     which is why `src/service.rs` labels `Runner.run | body` as a reference for
#     exactly the same shape. The replacement covers a qualified-name depth that no
#     other negative control reaches.
#
# **Seven `reference` lines were added**, and they are labelled additions rather
# than corrections: `render | label`, `render | count`, `format_line_inner | label`,
# `describe | label`, `describe | count`, `Ticks.save | count` and
# `Meters.save | count` are all uses of a name in a body, which is what a `reference`
# line has always meant here and what `src/model.rs` already labels at
# (`entry | label`, `entry | count`). Two of them — `render | count` and
# `describe | count` — were being resolved onto `format_line.count`, a different
# function's parameter, and **nothing could see it**: `references` was scored on
# whether a row existed and no line claimed these. A missing label is a hole in the
# population, which is the same failure as a wrong number in a different place.
#   * `reference | src/traits.rs | Saveable.save | entry` is believed wrong and is
#     **left alone**. `Saveable.save` has no body, so `entry` occurs only as the
#     parameter's binding, which this file's own negative rule says is not a use.
#     It is a miss rather than a violation, and correcting it would move a published
#     dimension to 100.00 in the same commit that changed the engine, so it is
#     reported instead.
#
# `binds` and `binds_nothing` below were added with the `resolution_correctness`
# dimension, and seven `reference` lines were added at the same time. The reason is
# recorded here because it is the same shape as the mistake this file exists to
# avoid: **`render | count` and `describe | count` were both resolved to the wrong
# entity for some time and no column could see it**, because no `reference` line
# claimed either row and `references` is scored on whether a row exists. A label that
# is missing is a hole in the population, so the seven field reads are labelled now,
# and `references` reads 25/26 rather than 18/19 because six of them were always
# there and the fixture simply had not claimed them. That is a **larger** denominator
# catching up, not a score going up.
#
# Format: `keyword | field | field | ...`. `#` starts a comment. Fields are
# trimmed. A field written `-` means "this one does not apply". An entity is named
# by `path | kind | qualified_name`, which is `EntityId` without the ordinal; an
# ordinal is only ever implied, where a source declares the same identity twice.
#
# Keywords and their fields:
#
#   symbol               | path | kind | qualified_name
#   member               | path | owner_qname | member_qname
#   call                 | path | source_kind | source_qname | callee
#   import               | path | local | item_path | alias
#   import_no_module     | path | local
#   reference            | path | source_qname | name
#   no_reference         | path | source_qname | name
#   inherits             | path | subject_qname | base
#   implements           | path | subject_qname | trait
#   no_inherits          | path | subject_qname | -
#   binds                | class | path | source_kind | source_qname | name | tpath | tkind | tqname
#   binds_nothing        | class | path | source_kind | source_qname | name
#   callers              | target_key | expected_caller_key
#   callees              | target_key | expected_callee_key
#   implementations      | target_key | expected_key
#   context              | target_string | expected_key
#   context_ambiguous    | target_string
#   floor                | dimension | basis_points
#
# `binds` and `binds_nothing` are the **placement** labels, and they are the only
# ones in this file that say where an edge must point rather than that it must
# exist. `binds` says that a *decided* relation naming that name has to point at
# that entity; `binds_nothing` says that no entity is the referent, so deciding
# the relation at all would be a false claim. A relation left undecided violates
# neither — that is a gap, and it is counted as one rather than scored.
#
# They name the relation they place with the **same five fields a `call` line
# uses** — class, path, source kind, source qualified name, callee name — so a
# placement claim can be read straight across from the `reference`, `call` or
# `import` line describing the same site, and a reviewer can check the two agree
# without translating between two vocabularies. The kind is on the line for the
# reason `call` puts it there: `counted` is both a macro and a function in this
# fixture.
#
# They are written out in full rather than folded into those lines, so adding a
# placement claim cannot move `references` or `calls` and the two questions stay
# tellable apart in a diff. The labelled relations carrying no claim are counted
# and printed, because a label nobody checked is not the same as a label checked.
#
# A relation can be labelled by both `reference` and `binds_nothing`:
# `summarise | out` is a use of the name `out` **and** has no referent, and both
# facts are true of the same line of source.
# `import`'s third field is the **item path as the source spells it**, not the
# module directory it came from: `use crate::model::{entry, Entry}` binds `entry`
# from `crate::model` and the item it names is `crate::model::entry`. Both spellings
# are checkable by reading the `use` line, and the item path is the one the source
# writes out in full.
#
# `symbol` is a multiset, not a set. Two `impl` blocks for one type produce two
# entities with the same path, kind and qualified name and different ordinals, so
# two lines mean two entities and the engine is scored on how many it emits.
#
# `floor` is a ratchet expressed as a percentage: `floor symbol_recall 73.12`
# means "recall must not fall below 73.12%".
#
# **Every floor is truncated, never rounded.** 87/90 is 96.666…%, which renders as
# `96.67`, and a floor transcribed from that rendering asks for more than the
# measurement is — so the gate fails on a measurement that has not changed. That is
# what a floor is: a lower bound, and the only safe lower bound at a given precision
# is the value rounded down. `Fraction::floor_spelling` produces these spellings.
#
# Every floor here is a measured value rather than a target, and `README.md` records
# the measurement each one came from. Raising one is a reviewable act that belongs
# in the commit which improves the number.
#
# Kinds are the engine's `EntityKind` spellings. An `impl` block is `module`
# because that is what the Rust spec maps `impl_item` to, and a `mod` declaration is
# also `module`; the spec tells them apart with `module_nodes`, not with the kind,
# which is why both spellings appear here.
#
# `member` means a member of a **type**: a field, a method, an associated type or a
# type parameter. A function parameter is a member of its function, which the model
# also says, but "does member resolution see through to the owning type" is the
# question E4 asks, so that is what is labelled here.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# src/lib.rs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
symbol | src/lib.rs | module | model
symbol | src/lib.rs | module | report
symbol | src/lib.rs | module | service
symbol | src/lib.rs | module | traits
symbol | src/lib.rs | constant | MAX_ROWS
symbol | src/lib.rs | static | RETRY_LIMIT
symbol | src/lib.rs | type_alias | Threshold
symbol | src/lib.rs | function | summarise
symbol | src/lib.rs | parameter | summarise.entries
symbol | src/lib.rs | macro | counted
symbol | src/lib.rs | function | counted
symbol | src/lib.rs | parameter | counted.body
symbol | src/lib.rs | module | outer
symbol | src/lib.rs | module | outer.inner
symbol | src/lib.rs | function | outer.inner.deep

import | src/lib.rs | Entry | model::Entry | -
import | src/lib.rs | Clock | traits::Clock | -
import | src/lib.rs | Saveable | traits::Saveable | -

# The three `pub use` statements bind local names to items in the modules this
# crate declares, and the items are here. `Renamed` is the alias case: `use
# crate::model::Alias as Renamed` binds a local name for a **type alias**, so the
# referent is `Alias` and not anything named `Renamed`.
binds | imports | src/lib.rs | file | lib.rs | Entry | src/model.rs | struct | Entry
binds | imports | src/lib.rs | file | lib.rs | Clock | src/traits.rs | trait | Clock
binds | imports | src/lib.rs | file | lib.rs | Saveable | src/traits.rs | trait | Saveable
binds | imports | src/report.rs | file | report.rs | Renamed | src/model.rs | type_alias | Alias

# `use` states in this crate root name the modules with a **relative** path rather
# than through `crate::`. Whatever a resolver does with that, the module the
# statement names is still in the source and still belongs in the graph.
import_no_module | src/lib.rs | Entry
import_no_module | src/lib.rs | Clock
import_no_module | src/lib.rs | Saveable

# One line per call site. `entries.iter().take(..).cloned().collect()` is four.
call | src/lib.rs | function | summarise | iter
call | src/lib.rs | function | summarise | take
call | src/lib.rs | function | summarise | cloned
call | src/lib.rs | function | summarise | collect
call | src/lib.rs | function | summarise | format_line
call | src/lib.rs | function | summarise | len
call | src/lib.rs | function | summarise | describe
call | src/lib.rs | function | summarise | render
call | src/lib.rs | function | summarise | push_str

# `counted!(body())` invokes the macro and invokes `body`. The macro body is a
# token tree to this grammar, so the inner call is not something an expression
# walker can see; the label is here because the source calls it, not because the
# gate expects the engine to find it.
call | src/lib.rs | function | counted | counted
call | src/lib.rs | function | counted | body
call | src/lib.rs | function | outer.inner.deep | summarise
call | src/lib.rs | function | outer.inner.deep | len

# Where the four must point. `iter`, `take`, `cloned`, `collect`, `len` and
# `push_str` are methods of `std` types: nothing in this repository declares any
# of them, so no entity is the referent. `deep` calls this file's `summarise`
# through `super::super`, and `counted!(body())` names the macro this file
# declares — the `body()` inside the token tree has no static target from an
# expression walk, and `binds_nothing` says so rather than claiming otherwise.
binds_nothing | calls | src/lib.rs | function | summarise | iter
binds_nothing | calls | src/lib.rs | function | summarise | take
binds_nothing | calls | src/lib.rs | function | summarise | cloned
binds_nothing | calls | src/lib.rs | function | summarise | collect
binds_nothing | calls | src/lib.rs | function | summarise | len
binds_nothing | calls | src/lib.rs | function | summarise | push_str
binds_nothing | calls | src/lib.rs | function | summarise | format_line
binds | calls | src/lib.rs | function | summarise | describe | src/service.rs | function | describe
binds | calls | src/lib.rs | function | summarise | render | src/report.rs | function | render
binds | calls | src/lib.rs | function | counted | counted | src/lib.rs | macro | counted
binds_nothing | calls | src/lib.rs | function | counted | body
binds | calls | src/lib.rs | function | outer.inner.deep | summarise | src/lib.rs | function | summarise
binds_nothing | calls | src/lib.rs | function | outer.inner.deep | len

# `kept`, `out`, `MAX_ROWS` and `entries` are uses inside a function body. A use of
# a name is a reference wherever it appears.
#
# `entries` is the parameter, and it is listed here rather than among the negatives
# for the same reason `src/model.rs` labels `entry | label` as a reference: a name a
# function's own parameter list binds is a use of that name from the body that reads
# it. The binding itself is the negative control, one line below.
reference | src/lib.rs | summarise | kept
reference | src/lib.rs | summarise | out
reference | src/lib.rs | summarise | MAX_ROWS
reference | src/lib.rs | summarise | entries
reference | src/lib.rs | counted | body

# Where those four must point. Read the source for each:
#
#   * `kept` and `out` are `let` bindings in the body of `summarise`. Nothing in
#     this repository declares either name, so a decided edge naming one would be
#     a claim about a declaration that does not exist. `binds_nothing` says so.
#   * `MAX_ROWS` is the constant this file declares.
#   * `entries` is `summarise`'s own parameter, and the use is in the body.
binds_nothing | references | src/lib.rs | function | summarise | kept
binds_nothing | references | src/lib.rs | function | summarise | out
binds | references | src/lib.rs | function | summarise | MAX_ROWS | src/lib.rs | constant | MAX_ROWS
binds | references | src/lib.rs | function | summarise | entries | src/lib.rs | parameter | summarise.entries
binds | references | src/lib.rs | function | counted | body | src/lib.rs | parameter | counted.body

# A declaration's own name is not a use of that name, and neither is the name of a
# declaration that encloses the use.
#
# The parameter's own binding is the occurrence the rule is about: `entries` appears
# twice in this function, once as `summarise.entries`'s binding and once as
# `entries.iter()`, and the first is a declaration while the second is the use
# labelled above. Naming the parameter here is what makes the pair checkable.
no_reference | src/lib.rs | summarise | summarise
no_reference | src/lib.rs | summarise.entries | entries
no_reference | src/lib.rs | MAX_ROWS | MAX_ROWS
no_reference | src/lib.rs | summarise | lib.rs

# The same rule three levels down, where no other negative control reaches: `deep` is
# declared inside a module inside a module, and its body names `super::super` and
# `summarise` but never itself.
no_reference | src/lib.rs | outer.inner.deep | deep

